From: William Floyd <william.floyd@modopayments.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 20:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] udev: trigger on change as well as add

A Bluetooth controller is published before it can be configured. One that
loads firmware spends the whole download inside hdev->setup() with HCI_SETUP
held, and the management interface deliberately hides it for all of that, so
every attempt fails with "Invalid Index". On a Samsung Galaxy S9 the
BCM4361B2 takes about ten seconds over it, which outlasts BT_TIMEOUT, and
the controller keeps the default address every unit of that model shares.

Retrying cannot fix this on its own: the rule fires at "add", which is
simply too early, and how long the firmware takes is not ours to know.
Raising the timeout would just be a longer guess.

Kernels that report readiness send a change event once a controller can be
configured. Match that too, rather than instead, so nothing regresses on
kernels that do not send it: there, "add" behaves exactly as before.

Reported upstream against systemd as issue #35003, closed as cant-fix --
udev had nothing to wait for.

Append SYSTEMD_WANTS rather than assigning it, too. Assigning replaces
whatever an earlier rule asked for, so a device that configures its own
controller from a lower-numbered rule silently loses it here and neither
unit runs: this rule wins the assignment, and if it is masked, nothing
starts at all. The variable is a space separated list and is meant to be
added to.
---
 bootmac-bluetooth.rules | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/bootmac-bluetooth.rules
+++ b/bootmac-bluetooth.rules
@@ -4,8 +4,15 @@
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
 #
 
+# A controller is published before it can be configured: one that loads
+# firmware holds HCI_SETUP for the whole download, and the management
+# interface hides it until that finishes, so "add" alone races the firmware
+# and loses. Kernels that report readiness send a change event once the
+# controller can be configured; match both, so this keeps working on kernels
+# that do not.
+#
 # If systemd is available, use unit activation
-ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="bluetooth", KERNEL=="hci0", TEST=="/run/systemd/system", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}="bootmac@bluetooth.service"
+ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="bluetooth", KERNEL=="hci0", TEST=="/run/systemd/system", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="bootmac@bluetooth.service"
 
 # Otherwise, run directly
-ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="bluetooth", KERNEL=="hci0", TEST!="/run/systemd/system", RUN+="/usr/bin/bootmac --bluetooth"
+ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="bluetooth", KERNEL=="hci0", TEST!="/run/systemd/system", RUN+="/usr/bin/bootmac --bluetooth"
